  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>&#10230;</mo><mi>Cu</mi></math>&nbsp; &nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></mfenced></math></p><p>According to Faraday&#39;s first law, when an electric current is passed through an electrolyte, amount of substance deposited is directly proportional to the quantity of electric charge passed through the electrolyte.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8757;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mol</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>63</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi></math> is obtained by passing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math> of electricity.</p><p>Mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi></math> obtained by passing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math> of electricity&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>63</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo></math> Mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi></math> obtained by passing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math> of electricity<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>63</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=